{"causes":["Maturinus was born in Gaul during a time of great turmoil, when the Roman Empire was under siege from internal strife and external threats. The persecution of Christians under Emperor Diocletian further exacerbated this situation, forcing Maturinus to flee for his life and eventually find solace in the Christian faith.","The rise of Christianity in Gaul was largely facilitated by the missionary work of Maturinus and other early Christian leaders. This led to a significant shift in the cultural and social landscape of the region, as Christians began to establish churches, monasteries, and communities throughout Gaul.","Maturinus's writings on exorcism and spirituality helped shape the theological debates of his time, contributing to a deeper understanding of the nature of evil and the power of prayer. His treatises also influenced the development of monasticism, which would become a cornerstone of Christian spirituality in Western Europe."],"effects":["Maturinus's legacy as a saint has endured for centuries, with numerous miracles attributed to his intercession. His influence can be seen in the establishment of monasteries and the spread of Christianity throughout Gaul.","The writings of Maturinus have had a lasting impact on Christian theology and spirituality, influencing the development of monasticism and shaping the theological debates of his time.","Maturinus's example has inspired countless generations of Christians to live a life of devotion, prayer, and service, cementing his place as one of the most revered figures in Christian history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"diocletian","sourceName":"Diocletian","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"As emperor, Diocletian's persecution of Christians led Maturinus to flee for his life and find solace in the Christian faith."},{"sourceSlug":"gregory-of-tours","sourceName":"Gregory of Tours","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"Gregory of Tours hailed Maturinus as a 'model of piety' and a 'glorious martyr' in his Historia Francorum."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-church","sourceName":"Christian Church","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"The Christian Church, under the leadership of Maturinus and other early Christian leaders, played a crucial role in establishing its presence in Gaul."},{"sourceSlug":"gaul","sourceName":"Gaul","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"Maturinus spent the latter part of his life on Lérins Island, off the coast of modern-day France, which was then known as Gaul."},{"sourceSlug":"monasticism","sourceName":"Monasticism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"Maturinus's writings on spirituality and his example helped shape the development of monasticism, which became a cornerstone of Christian spirituality in Western Europe."},{"sourceSlug":"exorcism","sourceName":"Exorcism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"Maturinus wrote several treatises on exorcism, which helped shape the theological debates of his time and contributed to a deeper understanding of the nature of evil."},{"sourceSlug":"lérins-island","sourceName":"Lérins Island","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"Maturinus spent his final years on Lérins Island, where he wrote several treatises and was revered as a saint by the local community."},{"sourceSlug":"gregory-of-tours","sourceName":"Gregory of Tours","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"maturinus","targetName":"Maturinus","context":"According to Gregory of Tours, Maturinus performed numerous miracles during his lifetime, which were later attributed to his intercession."}],"places":[{"name":"Lérins Island","role":"Birthplace and final resting place"},{"name":"Gaul","role":"Place of missionary work and establishment of the Christian Church"},{"name":"Roman Empire","role":"Context for Maturinus's early life and persecution"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Maturinus","type":"Reference article","slug":"maturinus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2658305","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maturinus"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Saint Maturinus (also Mathurin or Materne; died c. 388 CE, or by some traditions c. 301 CE) was a Christian confessor and saint of the late Roman period — associated with Larchant (Roman Larca/Larcha) in the Gâtinais region of what is now north-central France (Seine-et-Marne, Île-de-France). He is venerated in the Catholic Church and his feast day is celebrated on 1 November in some local traditions and on 9 November in the Roman Martyrology. Maturinus is the patron saint of Larchant and is traditionally credited with the evangelisation of the Gâtinais region, with exorcising the daughter of the Roman emperor Maximianus (a legendary element typical of early Christian hagiography), and with working miracles of healing.

The historical evidence for Maturinus is almost entirely hagiographic: his life is known primarily through the 'Vita Sancti Maturini' composed much later than the events it purports to describe, and the historical details (including the story of Maximianus's daughter) are standard hagiographic topoi. What can be said with more confidence is that Maturinus was venerated in the Gâtinais region from an early period — the medieval pilgrimage church of Saint-Mathurin at Larchant was one of the major pilgrimage centres of the Île-de-France, attracting pilgrims from across the region throughout the medieval period. The 13th-century Gothic church of Saint-Mathurin at Larchant, built to accommodate the pilgrimage cult, was a significant medieval architectural achievement whose imposing tower (now ruined) is still a regional landmark.

Maturinus became associated in popular tradition with madness and mental illness — as the patron who could cure insanity — and this association gave his name to the 'Maisons de Mathurins' (later 'Maisons des fous'), the French institutions for the mentally ill. The 'Mathurins' designation for religious orders who ran charitable institutions reflects the popular association between Saint Mathurin and charitable care of the afflicted. The Order of the Most Holy Trinity (Trinitarians), who ran ransoming houses for captives and were known in France as the 'Mathurins,' had their Parisian house near the church of the Mathurins that was associated with the saint's name.","subjects":["Early Christianity","French Saints","Classical Era","France","Medieval Pilgrimage","Hagiography","Late Roman Gaul","Saints"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Maturinus's legacy as a saint has endured for centuries, with numerous miracles attributed to his intercession. His influence can be seen in the establishment of monasteries and the spread of Christianity throughout Gaul.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
